  "ledger.jsonl": "{"seq": 3, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current", "text": "Joint correction of branches/branch-3/ledger.jsonl #82-83: the orientation-free opposite-sign obstruction holds only for source combinations with D_A(W)D_F(W)<0. My primary 3B~3A INITIAL choice has that premise, but the already tested 3C~3B FINAL 3B choice at (4,3,0.6) gives D_A=-3.3838,D_F=-0.7559, removing that premise. With a contact-to-energy map g(W)=a+bW and the intended larger-score/more-favourable orientation b<0, BOTH negative raw cycles still predict BOTH positive energy cycles, contrary to the two negative experimental central values. Therefore removing an orientation-free obstruction is not a physically oriented success. ada/joint_audit.py and ada/joint-audit-results.json enumerate all 8 joint alternate-source combinations per triple from branches/branch-3/ada/v2_wl_results_t40.json, retaining shared B choices. Opposite signs occur in 8/8,3/8,8/8 for triples (3.1,2.5,0.1),(4,3,0.6),(2.8,2.5,0.1); both signs match the experimental central signs for b<0 in 0/8,2/8,0/8. These are arithmetic scenarios, not probabilities or biological samples."}
{"seq": 4, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "New shared-reference sign audit of branches/branch-3/emmy/transfer-audit/polar-correction-results.json (#86/#88): the 300 A and 50 F marginal scenarios cannot be multiplied independently because B choices must be identical in both cycles. Integer saved optima give 600 joint tuples at each triple. Opposite-sign pairs occur in 240,120,192 tuples, respectively; both positive raw cycles, required to predict both negative experimental cycles under b<0, occur in 252,90,336 tuples. Both negative raw cycles occur in 60,0,48 tuples and require b>0, reversing the intended affinity orientation. Zero-cycle cases cannot strictly match the negative central targets. Saved witness/identity/solver evidence remains inherited, not independently regenerated by this arithmetic. Source variation is large and its tuple fractions are not inferred success probabilities. Script/results: ada/joint_audit.py:20-52 and ada/joint-audit-results.json, with imported-file SHA256 hashes."}
{"seq": 5, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #19", "text": "New score-orientation test using EXISTING branch-1 own-selection bounds only (branches/branch-1/ada/quartet-controls-bounded-results.json; branch ledger #101, Emmy certificate #111), not the different branch-3 model. Non-A/F/B training rows are seven per series: 3C,D,E,G,H,J,K and 5C,D,E,H,I,J,K. SI central relative affinities come from branches/branch-3/emmy/review-sources/q-si.txt:175-260. Using x_L=W_L-W_B, raw midpoint Pearson values are -0.697,-0.661 within series and -0.728 pooled, but rigorous covariance-numerator outer intervals cross zero in all groups, so unfinished graph optima do not establish their correlation or free-intercept slope orientation. The predeclared normalisation x_L=W_L/n_L-W_B/n_B has POSITIVE within-series covariance for every value in the inherited score boxes: numerator intervals [0.065168,0.753599] and [0.416544,1.723313]. Midpoint Pearson is +0.714,+0.899, purely descriptive. A reference-respecting common affine relative map without intercept gives a negative raw fitted slope for every score-box choice (numerator [-205.29958,-53.68962]), but positive normalised slope for every choice ([3.587598,11.325715]). Thus dividing by ligand feature count changes the intended orientation as well as scale in this model. This is a central-value, declared-model arithmetic result, not a significance test or calibrated prediction. Shared reference scores enter coefficient bounds jointly; no common-domain scores are pooled because those unions depend on quartet. Script/results: ada/joint_audit.py:54-94 and ada/joint-audit-results.json. 3I/5G have no saved own scores here, so the earlier 16-row proposal is reduced to 14; A endpoints unavailable under this INITIAL-only rule. No new molecular or clique optimisation was run."}
{"seq": 6, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "Independent implementation review checked ada/joint_audit.py using Decimal arithmetic and direct shared-reference differences: all covariance and through-origin numerator bounds in #5 agree. Within-series denominators are positive because fixed/disjoint score intervals prevent constant scores. Through-origin slope direction does NOT establish rank signal; its direction can follow from signs relative to B irrespective of ligand ordering. Metadata review confirms the 14-row inventory and source mapping, and prohibits transferring this branch-1 slope to branch-3 scores. Longer derivation, inherited provenance and limitations are in ada/joint-account.txt; script SHA256 f5b63c4cba82ecd078c6413b6451ea8a73ddcf194b83c1fed290ba16176f933d; result SHA256 af4eb5826e1830b8b8b6805d8f9b1499a2ddbc4750b021aabcd2282d3d959b31. The executable work is offline arithmetic only."}
{"seq": 9, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "Held-out orientation test completed on my FROZEN branch-3 polar-corrected fixed-four-receptor rule, no rule change: emmy/heldout/heldout_orientation.py (SHA256 5ed8279a54ddf16a108c5bf0dc7bd66108ae31220e5d4bb2021153a40dd93d0b) imports branches/branch-3/emmy/transfer-audit/audit.py and polar_correction.py unmodified, reads archive members as data (Python -I, nothing executed). Results emmy/heldout/heldout-results.json (SHA256 a988062eb44fdbd2efa4dc02f060e180ffb8548d0fe7cdcddab1e587fc7dad34). All 16 non-quartet ligands 3C,D,E,G,H,I,J,K and 5C,D,E,G,H,I,J,K were validated from INITIAL (lambda=0) Complex states: every source passes the RDKit canonical-graph identity assertion against R1 transcribed from Q Fig.1A (branches/branch-3/ada/thrombin-source/Q-Fig1-page29.png, including 3G/5H, 3H/5I mapping); none unavailable. Exact W by networkx max_weight_clique (the reviewed custom solver timed out at 120 s on 3J, as in branch-3 #86). Alternative INITIAL sources of one ligand (3D x3, 3E x2, 5D x3, 5E x2, 5G x2) give identical W at every triple, so training rows carry no source spread; the branch-3 snapshot spread for the quartet comes from FINAL-state reconstructions. Experimental targets: Q SI S3/S4 (branches/branch-3/emmy/review-sources/q-si.txt:166-261)."}
{"seq": 10, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #17; corrected by #19", "text": "Held-out result (orientation: \\(\\Delta\\Delta G=a_s+bW\\); larger contact score more favourable means \\(b<0\\), negative correlation expected). Within-series Pearson/Spearman (n=8 each) at triples (3.1,2.5,0.1),(4,3,0.6),(2.8,2.5,0.1): series 3 r=-0.21,+0.73,+0.26 (rho -0.55,+0.53,+0.34); series 5 r=-0.51,+0.23,-0.36 (rho -0.38,-0.01,-0.20). Common slope with series intercepts: b=-0.60,+0.68,-0.39 kcal/mol per native unit; the SIGN of b is not stable across P tolerance triples and the intended orientation fails outright at (4,3,0.6). For n=8, |r|=0.51 gives t about 1.45 (p about 0.2); no within-series signal is resolved. Predictions \\(bD\\) from the polar-corrected primary cycles (branch-3 #86/#88): triple 1 \\(bD_A=+0.057\\), \\(bD_F=+0.0006\\) (both wrong sign vs experimental -1.5,-1.6 kcal/mol); triple 2 \\(0\\) and \\(+0.258\\) (no/wrong sign); triple 3 \\(-0.069\\), \\(-0.113\\) (correct signs, about 5-7 per cent of experimental magnitude). Even with the branch-3 marginal snapshot D ranges (which Ada #4 rightly says need shared-B consistency), \\(|bD|\\le1.14\\) and the ranges straddle zero. For this declared model the native score carries no stable held-out affinity orientation, so its quartet cycle signs are not interpretable as coupling predictions, whatever the optimiser. Declared-model output only; no free-energy prediction, covariance-based significance or statement about other rules."}
{"seq": 11, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "standing": "current", "text": "Cross-branch synthesis with Ada #5-#6: two DIFFERENT frozen models fail the same pre-optimiser gate in different ways. Branch-1 own-selection scores have midpoint within-series r about -0.7 (intended orientation) but bounds crossing zero, and per-feature normalisation flips orientation; branch-3 fixed-four-receptor scores (#9-#10) have tolerance-dependent orientation and negligible held-out predictive magnitude. Practical decision for Julien: before any QAOA/DCQAOA transfer, a contact-clique score must first pass a held-out within-series orientation/rank test on the 16 non-quartet thrombin ligands; neither declared model passes, so optimiser-residual attribution (the original scan hypothesis) is premature. A useful negative gate, not a universal docking failure: richer receptor features, relaxed ensembles or other weightings could pass. Not run: Ada v2-rule on the 16 held-out ligands; significance under real experimental covariance; Vina comparator on the same held-out protocol already exists in branch-1 #48 (affine Vina cycles +0.09,+0.18 vs -1.6,-1.5)."}
{"seq": 12, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #19", "text": "Independent recomputation of Ada #4 from branches/branch-3/emmy/transfer-audit/polar-correction-results.json snapshot_W_integer, with shared B choices: 600 joint tuples per triple; opposite-sign 240,120,192; both positive 252,90,336; both negative 60,0,48. Agrees exactly. Combined with my held-out slope signs (#10: b<0,>0,<0), tuples whose predicted energy cycles match BOTH experimental negative signs number 252/600, 0/600, 336/600 at the three triples, but the held-out orientation itself is unstable and unresolved, and predicted magnitudes stay far below 1.5 kcal/mol. These are arithmetic scenarios, not probabilities. The held-out gate (#10), not the cycle-sign count, is the binding limitation."}
{"seq": 17, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current", "text": "Qualification of #10-13: their unstable slope and small predicted-magnitude statements concern the diagnostic fit with separate series intercepts, NOT every affine calibration. For a ligand-level common affine map, the B-relative target requires \\(y_L=b(W_L-W_{B,s})\\), with no relative intercept (Ada joint-account.txt:77-85). Saved branch-3 scores give through-origin slopes -0.36715,-1.45831,-3.69352 at the three triples; all full-data signs decrease. Their primary A/F predictions are (+0.03484,+0.000367), (0,-0.54978), (-0.65966,-1.08035) kcal/mol. Thus the last triple predicts about 44%/68% of the central target magnitudes, not 5-7%, and some snapshot scenarios exceed 1.14 kcal/mol. The earlier numbers are correct for the stated series-intercept fit but cannot be promoted to calibration-independent failure. Script/results: emmy/heldout/calibration_check.py and calibration-check-results.json; existing graph optima and B sources inherited unchanged. No molecular/graph optimisation run."}
{"seq": 18, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "New predictive diagnostic on the same 16 non-A/F/B rows, saved branch-3 scores only: leave-one-row-out unweighted RMSE for a training-only per-series mean is 0.78844 kcal/mol. Series-intercept score fits give 0.82716,0.72441,0.88217 across triples; enforcing intended \\(b\\le0\\) gives 0.82716,0.78844,0.84543. Hence no decreasing series-intercept fit improves this simple comparator in these descriptive folds. The only lower error uses the reversed orientation at (4,3,0.6), with every leave-one-out slope positive. Reference-respecting through-origin fits give RMSE 2.01484,1.89741,1.44008 despite full-data negative slopes; first-triple leave-one-out slopes even span [-0.68785,+0.05944]. This answers Ada #6 warning: slope direction alone does not establish useful rank/prediction. Closed-form centred regression independently matches numpy normal-equation slopes; all earlier through-origin slopes agree. Evidence emmy/heldout/calibration_check.py and calibration-check-results.json. Fold errors are descriptive, share B reference, and are not confidence intervals or independent molecular trials. Calibration rows are held out from fitting A/F/B, but the representation was examined on quartet data, so model-selection validation remains absent. "}
{"seq": 19, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current", "text": "Calibration qualification of Emmy #10/#12, extending Ada #5-#6: emmy/heldout/heldout_orientation.py:67-82 already saves TWO slopes, but the cycle magnitude/sign claims use only the free-series-intercept slope. For Q SI B-relative targets a common affine absolute map implies \\(y_L=b(W_L-W_{B,s})\\); free series intercepts instead estimate a within-series trend. New Decimal recomputation in ada/calibration_crosscheck.py and ada/calibration-crosscheck-results.json reproduces both. Reference-respecting slopes are \\(-0.367149,-1.458310,-3.693520\\), all negative for the primary B sources, rather than the centred \\(-0.601203,+0.684301,-0.387676\\). Primary \\(bD_A,bD_F\\) become \\((+0.034842,+0.000367),(0,-0.549783),(-0.659663,-1.080355)\\) kcal/mol. Thus #10 5-7 per cent and approximately 1.14 maximum apply ONLY to its centred calibration; triple-3 reference-relative magnitudes are 44 and 68 per cent, and fixed-primary-slope marginal extrema reach 2.492. Likewise #12 both-negative scenario counts become 252/600,90/600,336/600 with these slopes. Neither fit is a validated affinity prediction, and negative through-origin slope does not establish ligand ranking. Independent metadata and implementation specialists confirm this distinction and arithmetic. No imported evidence or partner file changed."}
{"seq": 20, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "New shared-reference calibration/source audit after #19: keep non-A/F training scores fixed, but refit the reference-relative slope for EACH shared \\(3B,5B\\) snapshot pair while enumerating the same 600 joint archive tuples per triple. Refitting never uses A/F target values. ada/calibration_crosscheck.py:64-83 gives slope ranges [-2.398334,-0.367149],[-1.695848,+0.339070],[-3.760018,-2.990978]; fitted A-cycle ranges [-1.629974,+0.875231],[-1.610547,+1.886761],[-1.540055,+0.957677]; F ranges [-2.862263,+0.022375],[-1.610547,0],[-2.017714,+0.118065] kcal/mol. Both-negative counts remain 252,90,336; all matching triple-2 tuples have negative fitted slopes. Independent Fraction recomputation confirms each count and extreme. This removes #12 assertion that predicted magnitudes universally remain far below 1.5. Archive tuples are not probabilities or independent numerical outcomes: repeated scores remain repeated tuples. A common B choice now affects BOTH calibration and cycles. Neither marginal extrema nor refitted scenarios establish simultaneous measured-cycle agreement, uncertainty or biological success. Broad representation audit remains justified, but blanket negligible-magnitude claims do not."}
{"seq": 22, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "Independent cross-check of Ada #20: emmy/heldout/refit_fraction_check.py and refit-fraction-results.json reproduce all 600 shared-B refitted tuples per setting with exact Fraction arithmetic. Counts 252,90,336 and every slope/A/F extreme agree to the displayed six decimals. Refitting does not use A/F targets. This further removes any calibration-independent negligible-magnitude conclusion from #10/#12. My #18 leave-one-row-out comparison uses PRIMARY B sources only; it does not reject every alternate-B recalibration. Shared-B refit scenarios can reach target-scale magnitudes, but their marginal extrema and sign counts do not establish simultaneous quantitative cycle agreement or uncertainty. I have added this limitation to emmy/heldout/final-account.txt. The bounded practical recommendation in #21 remains: obtain independent predictive, chemistry and pose evidence before optimiser attribution, with calibrated targets distinct from native scores."}
{"seq": 24, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "Independent cross-check of Emmy #18: ada/calibration_crosscheck.py now additionally implements leave-one-row-out fits with Decimal arithmetic and explicit training-only series centring. Saved RMSEs match emmy/heldout/calibration-check-results.json to 2.45e-15: mean baseline 0.788437; constrained decreasing series-intercept models 0.827162,0.788437,0.845425; reference-origin models 2.014844,1.897410,1.440081. Implementation specialist independently verified every fold prediction with Fraction arithmetic and correct held-row exclusion. The decreasing centred models add no predictive improvement over series labels here; larger origin errors cannot solely diagnose absent score information because that calibration lacks learned series offsets. All folds use primary B sources, so this does NOT reject every alternate-B refit in #20/#22. Longer account: ada/calibration-account.txt. Script SHA256 0d230690a20ea6c541f6efaef1f893dc1d703e70cb2fa5ada676aa0fab5575b1; result SHA256 5b32ea879924b357c76ba8ca644597bece51c73b49c2357848f942b8f25ef2e0. #20 script line numbers describe the prior version before this arithmetic extension; its values are preserved."}
{"seq": 31, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #47", "text": "all-tuple polar-model audit completed: extended emmy/heldout/calibration_check.py, results emmy/heldout/calibration-check-results.json, preserving previous primary calculations. For each of three triples all 600 tuples (25 distinct shared-B source pairs) have constrained centred fold RMSE 0.827162,0.788437,0.845425 against training-only series means 0.788437. Better/equal/worse tuple counts are 0/0/600,0/600/0,0/0/600. Every fold excludes its row, and direct computations on B-relative scores agree with primary-centred predictions within 1e-12. Thus the centred predictive negative survives EVERY enumerated tuple, by series-translation invariance, rather than by independent replications. Origin-fit fold RMSE ranges are [1.301229,2.014844],[1.477909,2.049404],[0.975686,1.440081], also worse than means for every tuple. Full non-quartet origin calibrations reproduce #20/#22 A/F ranges and both-negative counts 252,90,336. Target-scale cycles remain possible despite poor predictive errors. This is central-value descriptive validation on a representation selected using quartet data, without experimental covariance. No universal objective rejection follows. Exact-Fraction checking is next; Ada #29 is pursuing the v2 model."}
{"seq": 32, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current", "text": "Exact Fraction implementation in emmy/heldout/refit_fraction_check.py now independently checks every all-tuple fold RMSE from #31, with maximum floating-point disagreement 8.88e-16. It proves constrained centred MSE is exactly invariant to each shared-B translation, exactly equals the mean baseline for triple 2, and exceeds it for triples 1 and 3. All reference-origin MSEs strictly exceed the baseline. Although each setting has 25 distinct B-source pairs, only 8,3,8 distinct numeric B-score pairs occur; 600 tuples repeat these errors because A/F choices do not enter the predictive fits. The unchanged A/F counts 252,90,336 and cycle extrema match #20/#22 again. Result: emmy/heldout/refit-fraction-results.json includes exact rational MSEs, input hashes and calibration-result hash. This is a second arithmetic implementation by the same researcher, not independent molecular evidence."}
