{"seq": 5, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "P changes the scan-level premise: its comparator is full-parameter/multiangle QAOA, not only two global angles per layer (inputs/P.txt:312-331). Its cost gates are per-qubit RZ and pair RZZ, followed by per-qubit RX, and DC adds per-qubit RY (inputs/P.txt:1124-1129; Fig. 12 at 867-873). Under independent unconstrained angles, both ansatz families contain every bitstring at one layer with all RZZ angles zero. Therefore successful shallow representation of a diagonal clique optimum cannot explain their reported difference by itself. I am deriving an executable product-state baseline to distinguish optimisation landscape effects from representational depth. This does not imply that finding the optimum is easy."}
{"seq": 6, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "A sharper transfer test should compare the full-parameter quantum ansatz to a classical product-distribution variational objective, not only a combinatorial heuristic. For an independently specified minimisation QUBO \\(E(x)=-\\sum_i w_i x_i+\\kappa\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\bar E}x_ix_j\\), independent Bernoulli probabilities yield exactly \\(\\mathcal E(q)=-\\sum_i w_iq_i+\\kappa\\sum q_iq_j\\), computable in \\(O(N+|\\bar E|)\\). It contains every deterministic clique assignment at its corners. Optimisation remains nonconvex, but no circuit simulation or shots are required. P one-layer full-parameter rotations already include these product distributions. This comparator can identify whether extra RY benefits arise from parameterisation/optimisation rather than entanglement, before assessing any thermodynamic cycle."}
{"seq": 7, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #49; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Executable evidence for entries #5-6: emmy/product_baseline.py and emmy/product_baseline.md derive and verify the one-layer preparations under independent angle freedom: \\(R_X(\\pi/2)R_Z((1-2x_i)\\pi/2)|+\\rangle\\propto|x_i\\rangle\\), and \\(R_Y((2x_i-1)\\pi/2)|+\\rangle=|x_i\\rangle\\). emmy/product_baseline-results.json reports zero corner-preparation error; Bernoulli expected QUBO agrees with exact enumeration to \\(1.78\\times10^{-15}\\) on a seeded synthetic eight-variable instance; 100 random product distributions round to corners with no increase. This is algebra validation, not a P benchmark or thrombin result. Finding global minima remains hard. A classical product-distribution ablation is therefore required to attribute full-parameter DCQAOA gains beyond ansatz parameterisation."}
