{"seq": 52, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "objected to by #60; corrected by #61; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "progress, extending #44. Endpoint rule frozen in ada/endpoint_big.py (docstring): ligand L is taken only from a bound Complex/core pair L~X in which L is the INITIAL state, removing atoms whose MORPH.pert initial_type is du; coordinates and GAFF types come from SYSTEM.top/crd (read as data, Python -I, no archive code run). Under this rule all four F/B positions have a usable bound starting structure: 5F from 5Series/5F~5C, 5B from 5B~5A, 3F from 3Series/3F~3C, 3B from 3B~3A (top/crd only, no PDB). 3A and 5A occur only as FINAL states (3B~3A, 3D~3A, 3G~3A, 3H~3A; 5B~5A, 5D~5A, 5H~5A, 5I~5A), so their coordinates would be the partner ligand geometry with retyped atoms; under the frozen rule the A/B cycle is NOT constructible and I record that as a limitation rather than projecting. Caveats: these are FESetup minimised/restrained-equilibrated starting poses (inputs/Q.txt Methods, 10 kcal/mol/A^2 restraint), single snapshots, not ensembles or crystal endpoints. MORPH.pert lists only perturbed atoms (40 of 51 for 5F~5C), so types for unchanged atoms must come from the topology, which the script does. Feature rule (not RDKit, which is unavailable here): ligand HD = N/O bearing H; HA = any O or N of GAFF type nb/nc/nd/n2/ne/nf; HP = C bonded only to C/H, or Cl/Br; AR = 5/6-ring centroids of aromatic GAFF types; protein features from fixed residue templates (backbone N/O, polar side chains, aliphatic carbons, aromatic ring centroids); receptor points kept within 5 Angstrom of any ligand heavy atom (P rule, inputs/P.txt:358-366). BIG: vertex weight Table I, strict compatibility \\(|d_1-d_2|<\\tau+\\epsilon_i+\\epsilon_j\\) with \\(\\epsilon_s\\) for HD/HA-HD/HA vertices, three frozen parameter sets from P Appendices B/C/D. Exact solver: branch-and-bound over ligand points (a clique uses each ligand point at most once), with time limit and completion flag; no heuristic substitution. The exact run is in progress; results will be in ada/endpoint-big-results.json with N=n*m, density, S_L=-W and the raw cycle \\(S_{5F}-S_{5B}-S_{3F}+S_{3B}\\) (Q Eq.4 sign, #41). No result is claimed yet."}
{"seq": 55, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #58; objected to by #60; corrected by #61; corrected by #73; corrected by #74; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "result, completing #52/#53. Evidence: ada/endpoint_big.py, ada/endpoint-big-results.json, ada/endpoint-big-bounds.txt (archive read as data with Python -I; no new molecular calculation). Real thrombin F/B BIG sizes under the frozen rule: ligand points n = 17 (5F), 14 (5B), 17 (3F), 14 (3B); receptor points within 5 Angstrom m = 43, 40, 39, 41; qubits \\(N=nm\\) = 731, 560, 663, 574. That is about 50 to 120 times P largest demonstrated instance (12 qubits). Graph density at P parameter sets is 0.59 to 0.80, so the compatibility constraint barely binds. Exact branch-and-bound (120 s limit) completed for 5B and 3B under all three sets (Appendix B/C/D) but NOT for 5F and 3F, which are reported as incumbents plus a certified static upper bound, with no heuristic substituted. Key structural result: 3B exact optimum \\(W=4.5171\\) equals its geometry-free static bound (each ligand point given its best Table I partner type) under all three sets; 5B does too under Appendix B (4.9540). In that regime the score is a pure feature-type count sum, and the count-only cycle is EXACTLY zero: \\(-5.6491+4.9540+5.2122-4.5171=0\\). Additive type counting cannot produce non-additivity by construction. Any nonzero graph cycle must come from the compatibility constraint binding in specific ligands, as in the #25 mechanism. Raw dimensionless cycles \\(C=S_{5F}-S_{5B}-S_{3F}+S_{3B}\\) (Q Eq.4 sign, #41): incumbents -0.346 (B), +0.031 (C), -0.085 (D). Certified intervals [-0.432, +0.086], [-0.602, +0.548], [-0.699, +0.528] all contain zero, so the actual graph cycle is unresolved and its sign is undetermined. The X effect \\(S_{5B}-S_{3B}\\) is -0.437 (B) or -0.352 (C, D): it comes from the NH3+ adding an HD and removing an HP carbon, i.e. counting. Experiment is -1.6 kcal/mol (#16), but graph units are not kcal/mol and no calibration exists (#47). A/B is not constructible (#52)."}
{"seq": 56, "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "standing": "corrected by #58; corrected by #61; objected to by #68; answered by #71; corrected by #73; corrected by #74; answered by #119; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Limits on the #54 finding. (1) The feature rule is mine, not RDKit or P implementation (RDKit unavailable). HP counts are large (9-13 ligand carbons) because every C-only carbon is a point, whereas P hand-selected 2-3 ligand points (inputs/P.txt:358-366). P own reduction is not specified as a rule and would change n, m and density. (2) Single FESetup restrained starting snapshots, not ensembles, and they use the known pose for the 5 Angstrom receptor selection, as P does. (3) 5F and 3F exact optima are unfinished at 120 s, so the graph cycle sign is open. A longer exact run or a MIP solver is the next step, not a heuristic. (4) Density 0.6-0.8 at P thresholds suggests the published thresholds were tuned for very small, hand-picked point sets. At realistic point counts the BIG optimum approaches type counting, which supports #3 on real thrombin coordinates. This is conditional on the rule above and is not a statement about P software. (5) These graph sizes also mean the solver-transfer question in the scan premise does not arise at P demonstrated scale: about 560-731 qubits with dense penalty couplings are far beyond the 6-12 qubit simulations. No quantum resource claim is made."}
