{"seq": 60, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "objection", "standing": "answered by #62; answered by #64; answered by #66; answered by #69; answered by #76; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Cross-check of Ada #52/#55 exposes a domain distinction requiring correction before P transfer. ada/endpoint_big.py:119 forbids two vertices sharing either ligand or receptor point, and :124-141 branches at most once per ligand point. P published adjacency permits repeated ligand points: emmy/published_graph_check.py:9-12 has clique vertices (0,1,2), all three corresponding to the SAME selected ligand feature paired to different protein features. The Appendix B margin rule alone (inputs/P.txt:982 and 1135-1138; ada/P-author.txt:911-920) does not impose this injectivity. Therefore #55 graphs are a stricter correspondence domain, not a literal implementation of the published P graph rule. Their static type-count bound and zero count-only cycle depend on the added at-most-once constraint. I am checking this directly on transcribed published adjacency and on the frozen endpoint points; I will not modify Ada files. Please keep that possible domain mismatch explicit."}
{"seq": 61, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #73; corrected by #74; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Correction to #52/#55/#56: inspection of ada/endpoint_big.py:119-120 reveals an extra injectivity constraint: contacts sharing either ligand or receptor feature are excluded. This is NOT P Appendix B distance-only graph. Emmy published_graph_check.py:10-12 encodes the printed 8SKH top clique (0,1,2), all sharing ligand ha1, with weight 2.0058; P lists these contact names at inputs/P.txt:1145-1150 and defines only positive connectivity and zero diagonal at 1132-1141 (ada/P-author.txt:911-925). Thus the old thrombin results are for an independently modified injective-contact surrogate. Its geometry-free type-count upper bounds, zero count cycle and cycle intervals are valid only for that modified domain, and cannot establish P BIG type-count behaviour. The source citation in #52 did not disclose this modification. Preserve those files as earlier results; I will build a separately named distance-only graph and bounded general maximum-weight-clique search after specialist review. Q target, archived starting structures, feature-rule limitations and qubit counts remain as stated; actual P-style thrombin graph cycles remain unresolved."}
{"seq": 62, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #73; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Executable domain audit of #60/#61: emmy/contact_domain_audit.py ran with Python -I and reused only the reviewed function/constants AST of ada/endpoint_big.py (SHA256 recorded), reading archive coordinates/topology as data. It restores P distance-only compatibility on distinct vertices sharing ligand/receptor features; all other frozen feature/selection rules remain identical. Deterministic descending-weight greedy selections are saved and exhaustively checked pairwise as clique certificates, not exact optima and not cycle estimates. Under Appendix B, certified feasible weights in order (5F,5B,3F,3B) are (34.6817,34.0451,30.9872,32.6924), each exceeding the old injective-domain static bounds (5.6491,4.9540,5.2122,4.5171). Restoring repeated contacts adds respectively 11503,8321,10260,8864 edges; Appendix C/D certificates also exceed every old bound. Evidence: emmy/contact-domain-results.json. Thus the former bounds cannot constrain P-style graph scores, independently confirming Ada #61. No signed cycle of these greedy weights is interpreted as an exact cycle; no pose or thermodynamic inference follows. The distinction is multi-contact versus injective correspondence semantics, not automatically an error in P: multiple simultaneous contacts may be intended, but their physical feasibility and ensemble meaning still require validation."}
{"seq": 63, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Additional endpoint-chemistry cross-check of Ada #52: emmy/contact_domain_audit.py now parses Amber BONDS_INC_HYDROGEN and BONDS_WITHOUT_HYDROGEN directly, restricts to retained INITIAL ligand atoms, and compares those bonds with Ada distance-inferred neighbourhoods. For (5F,5B,3F,3B), retained bond counts are (52,43,49,40), with zero mismatched bonds and zero disagreement between active MORPH.pert initial_type and SYSTEM.top GAFF atom types. Evidence emmy/contact-domain-results.json chemistry_checks. This supports the specific initial atom/type/bond projection used for those snapshots; it does not validate the feature family definitions against RDKit, charges/valence completeness, protonation assignment, receptor templates, poses or ensembles. The A/B limitation remains a frozen INITIAL-pose rule limitation, not proof that no chemically projectable final endpoints could ever be obtained."}
{"seq": 67, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #73; corrected by #74; corrected by #84; corrected by #86; adopted by the joint ledger #13", "text": "result in the P distance-only domain, superseding the injective-domain cycle in #55 as the P-style answer (#61). Evidence: ada/scripts2/distance_only_big.py, ada/distance-only-big-results.json, ada/distance-only-big.log (Python -I via uv with numpy/scipy; archive read as data; only function defs/constants of ada/endpoint_big.py reused, SHA256 recorded). Appendix B parameters (\\(\\epsilon=4,\\epsilon_s=3,\\tau=0.6\\)), strict Appendix B margin, no injectivity, frozen #52 features and 5 Angstrom receptor rule. Exact maximum-weight cliques were obtained by HiGHS MILP (edge formulation), all four with status Optimal inside the 90 s limit, and each returned clique was checked pairwise as a clique. \\(W\\) for (5F,5B,3F,3B) = (41.0582, 35.8431, 36.8334, 35.2678) on graphs of N = (731,560,663,574) vertices, density 0.82-0.84. Clique sizes are (190,154,169,160) vertices; every ligand point is used, together with 26, 26, 29 and 24 receptor points. Raw dimensionless cycle \\(C=S_{5F}-S_{5B}-S_{3F}+S_{3B}\\) with \\(S=-W\\) (Q Eq. 4 sign, #41) = \\(-3.6495\\). Certified interval from the dual bounds: \\([-3.6495,-3.6491]\\), so the sign is resolved and negative, the same sign as the experimental \\(-1.6\\) kcal/mol (Q positive coupling). Decomposition: series-3 R effect \\(S_{3F}-S_{3B}=-1.566\\), series-5 R effect \\(S_{5F}-S_{5B}=-5.215\\), X effect \\(S_{5B}-S_{3B}=-0.575\\). Emmy greedy certificates in #62 (34.68, 34.05, 30.99, 32.69) are 6.4%, 5.0%, 15.9% and 7.3% below these optima, so the greedy cycle (\\(-1.35\\)) had the wrong magnitude. That is an instance of #11: per-ligand optimisation residuals do not cancel in the cycle. Graph units are not kcal/mol, and no calibration exists (#47)."}
{"seq": 68, "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "standing": "answered by #69; answered by #71; corrected by #73; corrected by #74; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Why the negative graph cycle in the preceding finding should not be read as P capturing thrombin coupling. (1) Extensivity: optimal cliques hold 154-190 vertices and reuse each ligand point with about 9-11 receptor points, so \\(W\\) behaves like a weighted count of mutually near ligand-receptor point pairs. At Appendix B allowances, pairs (l,p),(l,p\\prime) are compatible whenever \\(d(p,p^\\prime)<\\tau+\\epsilon_u+\\epsilon_v\\) (up to 8.6 Angstrom), which covers most of a 5 Angstrom shell. The score therefore scales with ligand point count n and receptor shell size m. (2) Pose-dependent domain: m comes from P 5 Angstrom selection around each ligand own pose (inputs/P.txt:358-366), giving m = 43, 40, 39, 41 here. The receptor vertex set thus differs by ligand. Part of the cycle may be selection-set size (5F has 17 ligand and 43 receptor points; its R effect of -5.2 against -1.6 in series 3 coincides with 4 more receptor points than 3F). This is a counting/domain channel, distinct from both the #25 contact-competition mechanism and physical cooperativity. I have not decomposed it; a fixed common receptor set (union over the quartet) would isolate it and is the next calculation. (3) One parameter set only (Appendix B); C and D were not run within this call. (4) Same limits as #56(1)-(2): custom feature rule (no RDKit; P hand-picks 2-3 ligand points), single restrained FESetup snapshots, known pose. (5) No physical pose feasibility (#18/#26): a 190-contact clique reusing ligand points is a multi-contact count, not a realisable correspondence. (6) Solver relevance: exact optima need 731-variable QUBOs that classical MILP solves in 10-75 s. That gives a concrete classical comparator at this scale and does not support a quantum transfer claim. Agreement in sign with experiment is a single quartet with no null distribution, and cannot be read as validation."}
{"seq": 73, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "cross-check of #55/#56/#68/#69 against ada/distance-only-big-results.json: in order (5F,5B,3F,3B), selected receptor counts are (43,40,39,41), used receptor counts (26,26,29,24), ligand counts (17,14,17,14). The simple claim that more used receptor points explains the larger 5F optimum is contradicted: 3F uses 29 against 5F 26, yet has lower W. The availability of different receptor identities remains a possible domain effect; counts alone leave that effect undecided. I withdraw the use of #55 density barely binds and #56(4) type-count behaviour as support about P-style thrombin graphs: those statements describe only the extra injective domain, as #61/#62 already establish. The negative graph cycle in #67 is dimensionless; its sign agrees with the negative experimental kcal/mol cycle, with no affinity calibration and no statement about magnitude."}
{"seq": 74, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "correction to #55/#56/#68/#69: selected receptor counts in order (5F,5B,3F,3B) are (43,40,39,41), but optimal cliques use (26,26,29,24) distinct receptor points (ada/distance-only-big-results.json). Both series ligand counts change 14 to 17. Thus the simple explanation that more selected or used points dictates the larger 5F weight is unsupported: 3F uses more distinct receptor points yet has smaller weight. Counts alone leave selection identity/type/geometry effects undecided; #68(2) is a hypothesis requiring the common-set control, not an explanation. Withdraw #56(4) as evidence for P distance-only type-count behaviour; #55 density-barely-binds and zero count-only cycle apply exclusively to the added injective surrogate (#61). #67/#69 agreement is sign only between dimensionless graph scores and experimental kcal/mol, with no calibration and no magnitude comparison."}
{"seq": 76, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Review of Ada #75 metadata/implementation/scientific scope: union by feature family plus residue/atom label, followed by evaluating those identities in each endpoint own receptor coordinates, is a coherent common-identity domain control. It removes ligand-dependent membership, while retaining receptor-conformation effects; call it common receptor identities, not a single fixed-coordinate receptor. Require all identities present once in every endpoint and deterministic ring labels/order. Under S_n=-W/n normalisation, n is positive and constant within each graph, so the same maximum clique optimises W and W/n and all dual bounds scale by 1/n; no new solve is needed. Strict distance-only compatibility must allow repeated endpoints, as #60-#63. Preserve all selected clique vertices to independently certify score and compatibility, distinguish solver status Optimal from a genuinely closed incumbent/dual interval, and report unresolved intervals. This control is about an explicitly custom contact objective, not P executable reconstruction or physical ensemble coupling. My failed specialist invocation had the same thread-store error; no separate specialist review is claimed. Appendix C/D F/B runs are in progress in emmy."}
{"seq": 79, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Review of Ada #77: a union of feature identities with all receptor coordinates taken from the 3B snapshot is a fixed-coordinate control, provided all identities exist in 3B. Its change from native own-selection scores combines membership and receptor conformation; do not attribute the difference solely to selection. A useful implementation simplification follows from P rule inputs/P.txt:1135-1138: compatibility uses only intraligand and intrareceptor distances. Therefore independently translating/rotating either point cloud leaves every edge and weight unchanged once the receptor identity set is fixed. Protein-CA alignment is necessary only for displaying/reconstructing a pose or redoing the 5 Angstrom selection, not for computing this union graph. Rigid alignment should leave the numerical graph unchanged apart from rounding. This is a source-rule invariance, not a new docking result; original ligand-dependent 5 Angstrom membership is the only rigid-pose dependence in that feature-selection channel."}
{"seq": 80, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Pre-solve validation caught duplicate receptor feature labels: protein_features names both TRP aromatic template rings TRP<residue>:ring. No optimisation had started. ada/scripts2/quartet_controls.py now appends a deterministic occurrence index in fixed protein-template order to every feature identity, preserving coordinates, family, number and weights. Union matching uses these identities and checks presence in all snapshots. Emmy #76 review is recorded; #77 additionally fixes common coordinates to 3B after CA alignment, so the control changes membership and receptor conformation jointly. C/D runs are left to Emmy #78 to avoid duplication."}
{"seq": 81, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "inventory before completed controls: INITIAL bound top/crd/pert endpoints permit C/B, D/B, E/B, F/B, 3G/5H/B, 3H/5I/B, J/B and K/B (eight matched quartets). A/B remains unavailable under the frozen projection. 3I and 5G both have initial files, but their matched R-group identity is not established by Q explicit caption mapping; no automatic remapping is asserted. Receptor identity unions validate after TRP ring disambiguation. CA alignment RMSDs are about 0.119-0.126 Angstrom for non-reference snapshots, recorded in ada/quartet-controls-results.json. Early 12 s MILP trials return wide bounds, so I will extend bounded solves rather than interpret unfinished incumbents as cycles."}
{"seq": 82, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Appendix B predeclared normalisation cross-check: using #67 saved MILP incumbents and n=(17,14,17,14), the S_n=-W/n F/B cycle is -0.20742479 dimensionless per-ligand-point score units; bounds are obtained by scaling each dual bound, not by comparing this number to kcal/mol. Appendix C 5F has hit 60 s with W incumbent 0.7561 and dual upper 27.0164, so this solve is unfinished and its weak incumbent is not an optimum. Previously saved pairwise-certified C/D feasible cliques from #62 can supply valid stronger lower bounds without replacing an exact cycle by a heuristic; all such intervals will remain explicitly unfinished. No dependence of physical NA on n is asserted."}
