  "branches/branch-2/ledger.jsonl": "{"seq": 2, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #7; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Supported derivation from the stated full-parameter circuit, pending primary-figure verification: inputs/P.txt:315-338 assigns a parameter to each gate and adds local Y rotations. With all ZZ, Z and X angles zero, a single layer is a tensor product of Y rotations on the initial plus states. Using standard rotation convention, \\(R_y(-\\pi/2)|+\\rangle=|0\\rangle\\) and \\(R_y(\\pi/2)|+\\rangle=|1\\rangle\\). Therefore every computational-basis clique solution is expressible at depth one; no entangling gate is needed to represent the exact minimum of any diagonal objective. This does not find the optimal bitstring: classical search remains hard. It does mean deeper-circuit success statistics alone do not establish counterdiabatic dynamics or a necessary depth resource."}
{"seq": 4, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Q supports a stringent coupling benchmark but not the claim that all docking objectives must fail. It reports Vina scores scattered around null coupling and an insignificant simulated F/B cycle (inputs/Q.txt:901-913), compensating S1/S2/Gly216 interactions (inputs/Q.txt:1010-1030), an A/B case with significant coupling despite no significant protein-ligand interaction-energy change (inputs/Q.txt:1033-1045), and insufficiently converged hydration components (inputs/Q.txt:1053-1063). These are source findings, not measurements made here. P’s clique optimiser does not evaluate these ensemble quantities."}
{"seq": 5, "actor": "ada", "kind": "idea", "standing": "corrected by #7; corrected by #26; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "The useful combined line may be an attribution benchmark with a compulsory separable classical control. Q supplies a binding-cycle target that plausible poses did not predict (inputs/Q.txt:666-678, :903-913, :1033-1068). P supplies a diagonal weighted-clique objective with a fully parameterised local-Y extension (inputs/P.txt:315-338). For a QUBO \\(E(x)=c+\\sum_i a_i x_i+\\sum_{i<j}b_{ij}x_i x_j\\), product states with Bernoulli probabilities \\(q_i\\) give exactly \\(\\langle E\\rangle=c+\\sum_i a_i q_i+\\sum_{i<j}b_{ij}q_iq_j\\), computable classically in polynomial time. This multilinear relaxation has the same global minimum as the discrete QUBO, since independent sampling gives a convex combination of discrete energies and every vertex is feasible. Local optimisation is still potentially difficult. Test whether DCQAOA improves cycle residuals over this exact same-expressivity separable baseline as well as classical clique solvers. This tests optimiser attribution and chemistry together, rather than calling clique ground-state fidelity biological accuracy."}
{"seq": 6, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Primary recovery provenance: APS article page https://journals.aps.org/prapplied/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevApplied.21.034036 verifies the 17 June 2024 figure-order correction, but the publisher PDF returned HTTP 401. Retrieved the author primary PDF via https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.04098 after metadata query https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2308.04098 identified v2, updated 16 May 2024. Saved ada/P-author.pdf and extracted ada/P-author.txt. This author version predates the publisher correction and is checked by content/location, not assumed to be identical to the corrected Version of Record."}
{"seq": 7, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #26; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Clarification of #2 and #5: depth-one exact representability is not unique to DCQAOA. In P’s full-parameter QAOA circuit, the independent Z and X rotations already suffice: with all ZZ angles zero, \\(R_x((1-2b)\\pi/2)R_z(\\pi/2)|+\\rangle\\) equals \\(|b\\rangle\\) up to phase for either bit \\(b\\). Thus both ansatz families can represent every exact optimum at one layer. The local-Y extension changes optimisation geometry and parametrisation, not the existence of a depth-one optimum. A matched separable control should include independent ZX and Y-only parametrisations, identical initialisation distributions and stopping budgets. No claim is made that either optimisation is easy."}
{"seq": 8, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "A sharper objective test is an invariance certificate, rather than interpreting any clique-score cycle as thermodynamic coupling. With fixed feature labels/weights, P replaces geometric mismatches by binary threshold edges. If geometry changes without crossing any threshold, the weighted graph and its exact maximum remain unchanged, although Q’s hydrogen-bond strengths and ensemble weights can change (inputs/Q.txt:1014-1045). Conversely an edge crossing can make an additive contact objective produce a nonzero substituent cycle solely through a changed feasible clique. I will calculate a small explicit example and state the graph-collision lower bound. This is a representation-level derivation, not evidence that the actual F/B quartet collides."}
{"seq": 9, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #26; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Executable evidence for #7 and #5: ada/separable_check.py was run with uv --cache-dir /private/tmp/q8p1-ada-uv run --no-project python ada/separable_check.py; ada/separable-results.json reports basis-preparation error 2.22e-16 and product-expectation error 1.11e-16. This is a synthetic four-variable algebra check, not a docking experiment. Primary circuit recovery is now verified visually in author PDF p.16, Fig.9 (ada/P-circuit-verified.png), and by ada/P-author.txt:956-988: independent rz0_i, rx0_i and ry0_i gate labels, Hadamard initialisation, and cost-before-mixer-before-Y ordering. Author text :246-267 confirms full parametrisation. Working derivation, assumptions, covariance formula and proposed matched controls are in ada/attribution.md. The publisher implementation and corrected PDF remain unverified."}
{"seq": 10, "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "standing": "answered by #12; answered by #15; answered by #34; corrected by #39; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Primary recovery also confirms a reproducibility problem that must not be silently repaired: author PDF text ada/P-author.txt:145-147 states compatibility with a greater-than-or-equal mismatch, whereas Appendix B :911-919 uses positive margin, hence strictly smaller mismatch. The same author PDF :237 states \\(x_i=(\\sigma_i^z-1)/2\\), which has eigenvalues 0 and -1 rather than the stated binary 0 and 1. A transfer implementation needs an explicitly documented canonical objective/sign convention and exhaustive bitstring checks. These are observed source inconsistencies, not evidence that the authors simulation used either erroneous rule. Emmy is independently investigating objective representation; my derivation applies to any correctly defined diagonal QUBO and does not depend on choosing a source correction."}
{"seq": 11, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Bounded prior-work search, not a novelty claim: web queries were \"QAOA counterdiabatic local Y product state classical baseline docking full parameter\" and \"multi angle QAOA one layer product states arbitrary bitstrings RZ RX\". They located the original multi-angle paper https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9043219/ and a later docking study https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.04239. API metadata https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2503.04239 (saved ada/prior-metadata.xml:10-22) verifies a 6 March 2025 preprint by Papalitsas et al., whose abstract reports GPU simulations of 14/17-node DCQAOA docking instances and increased computational time. Its full text and controls were not examined. I have not established novelty of the separable-control construction or of a thrombin-cycle benchmark. The later study makes a simple larger-instance replication an especially weak publication direction."}
{"seq": 12, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #39; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Current account and unresolved work: the scan question is sharpened from \"does stronger docking optimisation repair F/B coupling?\" to \"can an optimisation improvement survive both a separable classical attribution control and an independently calibrated physical-cycle test?\" Established here: both reported full-parameter ansatz families contain depth-one product-state exact optima (#7, #9); their separable expectations are classically computable (#5, #9); P contains primary-source encoding ambiguities (#10); Q distinguishes successful poses from missing thermodynamic coupling (#4). Emmy’s threshold-invariance line (#8) is compatible: a constant weighted graph necessarily has a constant exact score, but an actual thrombin collision has not been shown. Do not infer from either line that classical optimisation wins empirically. Missing: source coordinates and numerical supplementary cycle values with covariance; corrected publisher implementation/signs; frozen feature selection and score calibration; optimiser ablations, resource accounting and realistic-noise comparison. The paired-paper contribution is a falsifiable attribution benchmark, with a useful stop if exact score or matched separable controls fail. Publication potential and quantum benefit remain unassessed."}
