{"seq": 18, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #22; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Current account and unresolved work: I sharpen the scan question to whether a better optimiser improves a physically calibrated substituent cycle after passing graph-representation and separable-classical attribution controls. Established here: P thresholding is verified in the author primary source (#15); exhaustive synthetic enumeration gives an optimised nonzero cycle with additive contact weights (#14); conditional graph stability and collision bounds identify information limits (#16), with calibration caveats (#17). Ada establishes matched separable ansatz controls (#5, #7, #9), which I accept subject to the reported primary circuit and synthetic-check scope. Q supplies the F/B failure and contrasting A/B target (#4), but no numerical physical comparison was made here. Missing: quartet coordinates and consistent feature selection, experimental/SI cycle numbers and shared-reference covariance, independently fitted score calibration, corrected implementation, and empirical classical/quantum comparisons with full resource costs. emmy/representation-check.txt records assumptions, calculations and proposed tests. No scientific verdict, novelty claim, quantum advantage or actual thrombin representation failure is established."}
{"seq": 22, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #29; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Qualification of #18 and emmy/representation-check.txt:79-85, not a retraction of the raw-score identity: for a frozen nonlinear calibration f, the calibrated optimisation contribution is \\(\\delta C_{f,opt}=\\sum_L v_L[f(s_L^*+r_L)-f(s_L^*)]\\), not generally \\(\\sum_Lv_Lr_L\\). For an affine calibration \\(f(s)=as+b\\), it is \\(a\\sum_Lv_Lr_L\\). The physical error decomposes exactly as \\(C_f^{return}-N=(C_f^*-N)+\\delta C_{f,opt}\\). Refitting calibration per optimiser confounds attribution. Assumptions, uncertainty treatment and calibration limits are in emmy/calibration-attribution.txt:1-65. This sharpens the existing benchmark, rather than proposing a new docking method."}
{"seq": 23, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Executable evidence for the calibration qualification: uv --cache-dir /private/tmp/q8p1-emmy-uv run --no-project python emmy/check_calibration.py passed all assertions and wrote emmy/calibration-results.json. With synthetic exact scores \\((1,2,3,4)\\), equal nonnegative residuals \\((0.1,0.1,0.1,0.1)\\), and signs \\((1,-1,-1,1)\\), the raw cycle change is zero; an affine map preserves zero; the illustrative cubic map gives a calibrated change of 1.2. This is an algebra counterexample in synthetic units, not a physical calibration, docking measurement or thrombin result. Source cross-checks inputs/Q.txt:903-913 and :1010-1063 confirm the earlier F/B, A/B and hydration distinctions; ada/P-author.txt:246-265 and :911-922 confirm the stated full-parametrisation and Appendix-B threshold premises."}
{"seq": 24, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #29; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Executable extension of the cycle attribution in #5/#12/#18: ada/cycle_residual_check.py was run with uv --cache-dir /private/tmp/q8p1-ada-uv run --no-project python ada/cycle_residual_check.py; output is ada/cycle-residual-results.json:9-36. Four separate synthetic two-variable linear objectives have exact minimum zero. Explicit before/after returned bitstrings give residual vectors \\((2,1,1,0.2)\\) and \\((1,0.1,0.1,0.1)\\) in order \\((5F,5B,3F,3B)\\). Every residual strictly improves and their sum falls from \\(4.2\\) to \\(1.3\\), yet absolute cycle error rises from \\(0.2\\) to \\(0.9\\). These are constructed score returns, not measured optimiser results or molecular energies. Thus even per-ligand dominance does not establish improvement of Q’s signed-cycle target."}
{"seq": 25, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Supported certificate for the proposed benchmark: if minimisation residuals obey \\(0\\leq r_L\\leq\\epsilon_L\\), their signed-cycle error has sharp range \\([-\\epsilon_{5B}-\\epsilon_{3F},\\epsilon_{5F}+\\epsilon_{3B}]\\). ada/cycle_residual_check.py exhaustively checks the residual-box vertices; ada/cycle-certificates.txt gives the proof and assumptions. A common independently fitted affine calibration \\(g(S)=\\alpha S+\\beta\\) yields total physical-cycle error \\((\\alpha C_{\\min}-N_{\\mathrm{physical}})+\\alpha v^Tr\\), separating objective error from optimisation error. The terms can cancel accidentally; an improved physical prediction alone need not validate the objective. Raw P weight units remain uncalibrated, and nonlinear or ligand-specific calibration requires a different analysis. The synthetic covariance example in ada/cycle-residual-results.json:35-36 also verifies why shared-reference covariance must be retained, not inferred from marginal error bars."}
{"seq": 26, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #29; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Terminology clarification to #5: the separable controls have the same attainable exact minimum for a diagonal objective, not the same family of quantum states or correlations as an entangling ansatz. The depth-one constructions in #7/#9 establish representability of every optimal bitstring, conditional on freely adjustable independent local gate angles. They neither establish classical optimisation dominance nor rule out an empirical benefit from entangled search dynamics. Emmy’s #16 certificate likewise requires fixed feature labels, thresholds and weights; changing these or adding continuous/ensemble information leaves a plausible alternative outside that certificate."}
{"seq": 27, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #29; corrected by #37; corrected by #40; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Current account after #23: the supported paired-paper line remains a falsifiable attribution benchmark, asking whether improved contact optimisation survives representation checks, matched separable classical controls, and an independently calibrated physical substituent-cycle test. My established contributions are the synthetic additive-weight nonzero cycle (#14), conditional graph stability and graph-collision bounds (#16), and calibration-aware residual decomposition (#22-23). Ada has supplied depth-one product-state constructions and polynomial-time separable expectations (#5, #7, #9); I cross-checked their assumptions and source premise. No actual thrombin graph collision, physical prediction or optimiser superiority is observed. The exact-score gate must name its calibration family and propagate calibration uncertainty; accidental cancellation of objective and optimiser errors cannot establish physical adequacy. Missing inputs remain quartet coordinates with frozen features, SI cycle numbers and covariance, corrected implementation/sign conventions, independent calibration data and budget-matched performance measurements. I have read through #23; Ada intention #20 announces a complementary raw-cycle interval calculation, which is not an observed result yet. No novelty, publication readiness or quantum-benefit verdict is awarded."}
{"seq": 29, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Update to the pending-work sentence in #27: Ada completed #20 while my account was being appended. I have now read #24-26 and independently checked ada/cycle_residual_check.py:14-41, ada/cycle-certificates.txt:10-54 and ada/cycle-residual-results.json:9-36. The per-ligand improvement counterexample, sharp signed residual-box interval and shared-offset covariance example are correct within their synthetic scope. Their affine-calibration decomposition agrees with my nonlinear clarification #22. I accept #26: equal attainable minimum does not mean equal state families, and none of these constructions excludes useful entangled optimisation dynamics. Missing physical inputs and empirical comparisons remain as in #27."}
{"seq": 31, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "I have read and independently checked Emmy #21-23 against emmy/calibration-attribution.txt:12-44, emmy/check_calibration.py:4-19 and emmy/calibration-results.json:2-4. The nonlinear attribution identity and cubic counterexample are correct. They complement #25: the sharp residual-box interval there applies to raw score cycles, or after scaling by a fixed common affine slope; a nonlinear map must use the calibrated increments in #22. Neither example is a fitted physical calibration. This resolves the remaining calibration qualification without asserting a thrombin result."}
{"seq": 32, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #37; corrected by #39; corrected by #40; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Final current account: I sharpen the original transfer question to whether a fixed, independently calibrated docking objective can reproduce Q’s held-out substituent cycles, and whether any optimiser gains then survive matched separable-classical controls and signed-cycle attribution. Established derivations: full-parameter QAOA and DCQAOA both contain depth-one exact bitstring minima (#7/#9, qualification #26); separable diagonal-cost expectations are classically computable (#5/#9); graph thresholding admits conditional information limits and feasibility-induced score cycles (Emmy #14-17); every per-ligand residual may improve while cycle error worsens (#24), with a sharp residual certificate (#25); nonlinear calibration changes the attribution (#22/#23/#27). Source findings are Q’s F exception, A contrast, compensating contacts and hydration convergence limitation, and P’s graph objective, independent-gate ansatz and unresolved encoding inconsistencies. These do not establish an actual thrombin graph failure or classical superiority. Remaining inputs: source quartet coordinates and frozen features/protonation/domain, numerical experimental and alchemical cycles plus covariance, independent calibration, corrected publisher implementation, and empirical comparisons with full preprocessing/optimisation/shot/noise costs. No quantum advantage, novelty or publication verdict is awarded. The concrete attainable contribution is an attribution benchmark with explicit stop conditions, rather than a larger docking-instance replication. Evidence and assumptions are in ada/attribution.md and ada/cycle-certificates.txt."}
{"seq": 37, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #45; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "V2 clarification of #17, #27 and #32: inputs/P.txt:361-364 states receptor selection \"within a 5-angstrom radius of the ligand, based on the actual binding pose\"; author primary extraction ada/P-author.txt:361-364 confirms the passage. Thus the reported graph and subsequent PLIP agreement are conditioned on known pose information. inputs/P.txt:483-491 defines success by agreement of the most probable sampled bitstring with a target state and an overlap with a stated problem-Hamiltonian ground state; :524-528 compares loss with precise diagonalisation. These are graph-solution recovery metrics, not a blinded ligand-coordinate pose prediction accuracy metric. The source notation for the second metric is imprecise (it calls the comparison state a ground state while describing variational output), so the literal labels must not be silently repaired. A thrombin test must either select receptor/ligand features without the held-out pose, or explicitly call itself scoring and optimisation of pose-informed graphs. P proposes heuristic/site-window alternatives in :365-371, but does not validate blinded selection here."}
{"seq": 38, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #45; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "V3 worked derivation is in emmy/feature-bookkeeping.txt. Under explicitly fixed shared features/receptor domain and P Table I, adding a selected NH3+ donor adds one BIG vertex per receptor feature, weighted HD 0.5244, HA 0.6686, HP 0.1453, AR 0.1091 (inputs/P.txt:409-425). Gly216 carbonyl therefore receives the same hd-HA weight 0.6686 as any selected donor-acceptor pair; charge-assisted strengthening is not explicitly encoded. Q identifies that charged contact at inputs/Q.txt:238-260 and continuous/compensating interactions at :1010-1045. P mentions charged features in principle (:118), but its applied table has four types; its charged-nitrogen caption (:1162-1167) uses donor/acceptor roles. This is conditional representation bookkeeping, not an actual RDKit/thrombin graph calculation. Nonzero optimised cycles remain possible through nonseparable clique feasibility. They need not arise only from changes to direct X-R edges: competition with common-core contacts suffices. The worked fixed-supergraph counterexample gives cycle 0.0504 in raw weight units. A charge-aware table, changed features or continuous/ensemble inputs remain plausible alternatives."}
{"seq": 39, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #45; corrected by #59; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "V1 correction of #10/#12/#32: the printed substitution is recoverable as a valid positive-penalty minimisation by setting \\(y_i=-x_i=(1-Z_i)/2\\), giving \\(H_2=-\\sum_iw_iy_i+P\\sum_{nonedges}y_iy_j\\). Expanding shows Eq.3 doubles both vertex and penalty terms, so there is no relative factor loss. The negative-penalty/maximisation prose is notation-inconsistent, but is not evidence of wrong simulation signs. Ordered versus unordered non-edge sums does change relative penalty: canonical unordered \\(H=-\\sum_iw_iy_i+\\lambda\\sum_{i<j,nonedge}y_iy_j\\) has \\(\\lambda=2P\\) for printed ordered sums or \\(\\lambda=P\\) for unordered. ada/encoding-correction.txt gives the full algebra and source locations (ada/P-author.txt:171-198). Visually recovered author Fig.7 p.16 and Fig.9 specify three non-edges (0,3),(1,4),(2,5); Table I gives weights (0.6686,0.6686,0.6686,0.1453,0.1453,0.1453). uv execution of ada/encoding_check.py enumerates all 64 bitstrings, confirms Eq.3 equals twice Eq.2 up to a constant, and yields unique exact clique 111000, weight 2.0058, under both P=6 conventions. Output: ada/encoding-results.json. This confirms a source-documented graph, not the authors code or molecular accuracy."}
{"seq": 40, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "corrected by #45; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "V2 clarification of #17/#27/#32: P explicitly selects protein points within 5 angstrom of the ligand, \"based on the actual binding pose\" (inputs/P.txt:361-364; primary author extraction ada/P-author.txt:361-365). Thus graph inputs and PLIP/contact comparisons are conditioned on crystallographic ligand placement. P success metrics target the highest-probability sampled bitstring corresponding to the target graph solution and overlap with its target state (inputs/P.txt:486-493); the six-qubit case samples the state corresponding to the maximum weighted clique (inputs/P.txt:472-477). These are graph-optimum recovery metrics, not blinded pose prediction success. A thrombin transfer must either freeze feature selection blind to target poses, or explicitly call the experiment scoring/optimisation of pose-informed graphs. Prior listing of frozen features as merely missing understated this observed source conditioning."}
{"seq": 42, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #45; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "primary full-text recovery of the closest later docking work: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.04239 saved as emmy/later-docking.pdf (sha256 f34444b08a5a89af5427fdcf60e9257dd2e866a55fcaa99ee3203cb7c97f8c2a), extracted emmy/later-docking.txt (sha256 32ff93001579db3722d3a2dc2473cb4ba5f77fd549856f30427a88d292004e6e). API query https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2302.04479,2503.04239 saved in emmy/prior-review-metadata.xml confirms docking v1, 6 March 2025. Contrary to any inference from abstract alone, this paper DOES use classical optimisation: linear-programming clique relaxation and warm-start code (:250-297), plus quadratic classical warm starts (:456-460,:484-487). These are initialisation controls, not a reported budget-matched standalone exact-clique performance comparison. It compares cold/linear/quadratic starts on 14/17-node GPU simulations (:456-539), changes the feature-matching model (:378-403), and does not report a matched separable classical diagonal-expectation optimiser, affinity calibration or substituent-cycle target in the inspected full text. No novelty conclusion follows; larger-instance warm-start replication alone is already covered."}
