{"seq": 29, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "Closing account after reading Ada #23-25: #23 correctly makes the witness domain explicit and its signed interval does not require independent ligand gaps. This call independently checked the geometric constants (#27 corrected by #28) and established finite-ensemble non-identifiability with executable evidence (#26). Together with existing independent-angle basis-state reachability (#8) and graph/optimiser attribution (#18), the useful line is an objective-and-domain audit of the thrombin substituent cycle before quantum optimiser transfer. Additive weights can still give constraint-induced coupling (#17), and restricted-domain empirical calibration remains a live alternative (#26). The synthetic ensemble argument is standard and is not itself claimed as publication novelty. Missing actual thrombin quartet graphs, joint-pose certificates, normalised bound/water ensembles and independent calibration, experimental numerical covariance, implemented P conventions and final publisher PDF, matched classical optimisation budgets and novelty research prevent a biological result or publication verdict. No campaign, docking or quantum optimiser was launched in this call."}
{"seq": 34, "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "clarifies #18 and #23: define a fixed shot budget and accept only sampled bitstrings that induce a valid BIG clique. Include the empty clique as score-zero fallback; take T_L as the largest unpenalised vertex-weight sum among accepted samples. Report the invalid fraction separately, with no silent repair. Then T_L<=W_L and e_L=W_L-T_L>=0, including all-invalid batches. The identity D(T)=D(V)+D(g)-D(e) is unchanged, and #23 bounds only g, independently of this choice. For a single-draw or mean-valid-draw metric the same identity holds after defining T consistently, but it is a different estimand; do not compare its e with best-of-budget e. A raw invalid string cannot enter T via its unpenalised sum. Emmy #12 residual example is compatible with the valid-output minimisation convention S=-T, S_min=-W: r=S_returned-S_min=e, so delta C=D(e) while D(T) differs from D(W) by -D(e). Its numbers illustrate cancellation, not a sampled experiment. Literal incorrectly signed penalised objectives instead require a distinct objective optimum and cannot reuse W or the nonnegative clique gap without validation."}
{"seq": 35, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "clarification of Ada #18/#23 and Emmy #12: define a fixed shot budget M and retain only sampled strings whose selected vertices are valid cliques in the common BIG. Include the empty clique as a fallback, and let T_L be the largest sum of vertex weights among those retained strings, with a fixed lexicographic tie rule. Do not score invalid strings as unpenalised contacts. Report the invalid-shot fraction and probability of at least one nonempty valid sample separately. Then T_L <= W_L, e_L=W_L-T_L >= 0 and D(T)=D(V)+D(g)-D(e) remain exact, conditional on the realised batch. T is a random best-valid-sample statistic, not the modal bitstring or mean Hamiltonian energy used elsewhere in P. For a single-shot or averaged statistic the same identity holds per shot or after expectation if invalid outputs are mapped to the empty clique; it is a different estimator and must be labelled. An all-invalid batch therefore has T_L=0, with its optimiser-plus-sampling loss included in e_L. Shot counts and validity filtering belong in resource accounting. Emmy #12 residual cancellation example is compatible with this convention after consistent orientation S_L=-T_L, S_min,L=-W_L, so r_L=e_L; it was an algebraic possible residual vector, not an observed batch. Its minimisation cycle residual is +D(e), whereas the returned maximisation-score cycle contains -D(e). Penalised illegal-bitstring energies require a separate objective/domain attribution, not this clique-score identity."}
{"seq": 36, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "primary SI recovered through public Figshare resource-DOI API https://api.figshare.com/v2/articles?resource_doi=10.1021/acs.jpcb.6b03296, identifying records 3436451 and 3436448. Record https://api.figshare.com/v2/articles/3436451 supplies https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5385149, stored emmy/review-sources/jp6b03296_si_001.pdf (502899 bytes; SHA256 ca742f5de24570841aa6be41c646556bc5a14757eca6a705b1dc8b8a3eef871b). Record 3436448 supplies associated input archive https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5385146, stored emmy/review-sources/jp6b03296_si_002.zip (35284797 bytes; SHA256 064086b03aaed9f0efea1be198904ae71751d1fa0454086ac2e0d3e2001e33cc). No archive contents have yet been inspected or simulations launched. Direct ACS SI URL returned 403; a guessed unrelated Figshare download returned empty HTTP202 and is explicitly not evidence. Derived SI text emmy/review-sources/q-si.txt will be checked against the PDF. This recovery may remove part of the missing-input gate in #29; covariance and mappings still require inspection."}
{"seq": 37, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "current; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "prior-work check: Kuhl/Crippen/Friesen 1984 DOI https://doi.org/10.1002/jcc.540050105 publisher abstract describes contact/no-contact clique matching but gives no explicit sufficiency limitation. Full PDF https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/jcc.540050105 failed HTTP403, so its full treatment is unavailable here. Banchi et al. primary author PDF https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.00462 recovered after metadata API and 3-second interval, stored emmy/review-sources/banchi-primary.pdf SHA256 dad60f6dc2d5ef7102cda0a2f00d94e28486ba4caa7db37584e4de058f555b99. In Section II.D, derived emmy/review-sources/banchi-primary.txt:264-301, they state true poses give pairwise-compatible cliques; Fig.2 caption and :322-324 then associate highest-weight cliques with likely poses. Searches clique, rigid, rotation, realisable, realizable, fit, compatibility, distance, joint and superposition found no explicit certificate that every clique admits a joint pose. This is absence in inspected passages, not proof of no caveat elsewhere. Hu/Lill PharmDock 2014 primary https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1758-2946-6-14, section Pharmacophore-based pose sampling and ranking, explicitly follows clique detection with Kabsch alignment and steric rejection. Stored publisher HTML emmy/review-sources/pharmdock.html SHA256 7a0aa1e879c59636f63bb0cecaa4f4fd38a76427a7d3efa1d17b86696c6465f2. This establishes precedent for treating cliques as pose candidates needing geometric checks, although it does not state our repeated-contact tetrahedral theorem. FLAP 2007 DOI https://doi.org/10.1021/ci600253e search surfaced an explicit mismatch-between-distance-match-and-superposition passage, but primary full-text retrieval failed HTTP403; treat that as an unverified lead. Queries: distance geometry clique matching pairwise distances rigid superposition chirality pharmacophore docking; A Common Reference Framework superposition tetrahedra. Thus #15 and #18 should be framed as an audit applied to P/Q, not a newly discovered general limitation of geometric docking. Novelty of the quartet attribution and certification protocol remains unestablished."}
{"seq": 38, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #46; objected to by #47; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "independently checked with emmy/check_contact_windows.py and emmy/check_contact_windows_results.json: at zero tau, regular tetrahedron side 5.1 Angstrom has four-contact enclosing radius 3.123099422, so ball radius epsilon=3 fails while the H-bond window [2.5,3.3] succeeds at the centroid. Side 5.4 gives radius 3.306811153, exceeding the window upper bound 3.3, so both ball radius3 and that window fail despite every Appendix-B pairwise edge at epsilon3. Every triple at side5.4 has radius3.117691454 and a centroid window witness; therefore even triple window feasibility does not imply joint four-contact feasibility. At side5.1 triples fit both semantics (radius2.944486373). These conclusions use exact enclosing-radius certificates or explicit equidistant witnesses, not a failed local fit. Triangle hydrophobe [3.3,4] and aromatic [3.5,4.5] windows have explicit perpendicular-lift witnesses; certain tetrahedral cases for these wider windows remain unknown where the script finds no axial witness and has no certificate. Appendix-B with epsilon equal to a maximum radius follows as a non-strict necessary triangle-inequality condition, but its strict edge inequality excludes exact boundary contacts at tau0. With epsilon a typical value below the window maximum it need not be necessary: two receptor points separated6.4 admit distances3.2 at their midpoint in [2.5,3.3], yet epsilon3,tau0 rejects the pair. Positive tau restores this particular pair if tau>0.4, not universal necessity. Thus #11/#15/#27 ball-based claims cannot silently be carried to typical-distance semantics. New checks are synthetic, not P/Q pose results; hashes follow in the provenance entry."}
{"seq": 39, "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "standing": "corrected by #46; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "visually verified Q publisher SI Tables S3/S4 (PDF pages S7/S8; derived emmy/review-sources/q-si.txt:166-261). Relative-to-B experimental/predicted values in kcal/mol: 3A=0.7+/-0.4 / 0.59+/-0.02; 5A=-0.8+/-0.1 / 0.0+/-0.2; 3F=-0.8+/-0.2 / -1.70+/-0.07; 5F=-2.4+/-0.2 / -1.7+/-0.1; both B reference rows are0+/-0 by definition. Hence D_F experimental=-1.6 and computed=0.00; D_A experimental=-1.5 and computed=-0.59. Assuming independent series-level relative estimates, propagated displayed errors are0.28284/0.12207 for F and0.41231/0.200998 for A. This is a declared independence calculation, not recovered covariance: B reference zero does not imply independently measured absolute reference affinity has zero uncertainty. Without covariance, conservative correlation-compatible standard-deviation ranges are [0,0.4]/[0.03,0.17] for F and [0.3,0.5]/[0.18,0.22] for A, treating table errors as standard deviations. Q body inputs/Q.txt:1028-1045 identifies A R1=H and F R1=i-butyl; B identity not yet verified against its structure. Public Bristol AAM https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/files/92682149/JPCB2016_thrombin_nonadditivity_OA.pdf was readable via web PDF text but direct download403 and figure screenshot failure prevented visual Fig1 recovery. Baum primary https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmb.2010.02.007 abstract is accessible; ligand-to-PDB assignments remain unavailable here. The recovered archive contains input systems and scripts, so access is no longer an absolute absence; deterministic pharmacophore selection, point reduction, contact tolerances and P implementation conventions remain prerequisites to a uniquely specified exact W_L. No thrombin BIG or native cycle has been computed in this call."}
{"seq": 40, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "objected to by #47; corrected by #54; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "(targets #11, #14, #15, #27): stored ada/check_semantics.py (SHA256 f25ab219402988480b6cba6b7b4ba1733f231f92e34f4c7420276d9da0aa5ab0) and ada/check_semantics_results.json (SHA256 da7c96421c847fa23fea5fb9ee6c6d8d33f1b506dbfc19e9bb690df26d455edd), synthetic part :17-203. One ligand point, regular triangles and tetrahedra of protein points, two semantics: ball \\(|x-p_j|\\le\\epsilon=3\\) Angstrom, and window \\(2.5\\le|x-p_j|\\le3.3\\) Angstrom (P H-bond range, inputs/P.txt:196; hydrophobic 3.3-4 :208, pi 3.5-4.5 :211). Enclosing radii match closed forms \\(s/\\sqrt3\\), \\(s\\sqrt6/4\\) to 1e-6. Ball: Appendix-B pairwise test (\\(\\tau=0\\)) passes in every case; triangle s=5.4 and tetrahedron s=5.4 fail at triples; tetrahedron s=5.1 passes all triples and fails the four-set (#15 reproduced). Window: upper-bound obstruction persists after rescaling (triangle s=5.8, \\(R=3.3486>3.3\\), pairs feasible; tetrahedron s=5.4, triples feasible, four-set \\(R=3.3068>3.3\\)), but the specific #15 example s=5.1 becomes feasible because \\(R=3.123\\) lies in the window. A new lower-bound (clash-type) obstruction appears: compact tetrahedra s=2, 3, 4 Angstrom pass the ball test and have window witnesses for every pair and triple, but no window witness for all four (minimum violations 0.26, 0.47, 0.05 Angstrom; for s=4 it equals \\(2.5-R\\), the centroid). These are multistart pattern-search non-witnesses, not certificates, except where the closed-form radius decides. Necessity: by the triangle inequality the Appendix-B test with \\(\\epsilon_A,\\epsilon_B\\) is necessary under ball semantics with those radii and under window semantics only if each \\(\\epsilon\\ge\\epsilon_{\\max}\\) for its type; lower bounds add no pairwise condition for a repeated ligand point (two protein points at separation \\(\\le2\\epsilon_{\\max}\\) always admit a window point). With P actual parameters (\\(\\epsilon_s=2.5\\) or 3 below \\(\\epsilon_{\\max}=3.3\\)) the test is not necessary for window realisability: e.g. at 3HAC settings, two acceptors 5.1-6.6 Angstrom apart are window-realisable from one donor but get no edge. So under window semantics P graph can both over-admit (higher order) and under-admit (pairwise)."}
{"seq": 41, "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "standing": "objected to by #47; corrected by #54; not cited by the joint ledger", "text": "(targets #15, #18, #23, #24): retrieved https://files.rcsb.org/download/8SKH.pdb (SHA256 6fba529e599485c91af23fe3fe4a2e2dc84aa1bc7f1cd82a173b5b118a0a433e), https://files.rcsb.org/download/3HAC.pdb (589faeeff47a05421e48f83288cd3bf2a7a5d8743cacb3e60c4e6fa486244fe2), https://files.rcsb.org/download/5F4L.pdb (2557b96222020a0ab96ea44e05b9ec13ba40299b3d5f49d2d15053a38f6dd456) into ada/structures/; serials resolved with ada/scripts/resolve_serials.py (SHA256 97ebfd23b6a541dcb173eeedbca9c658c372a1bfb544846b800d1e22fa6768ca). All quoted serials exist. Label discrepancies: 3HAC \"C5171 (HA3)\" is TYR A662 OH and \"01453\" is GLU A205 OE2 (inputs/P.txt:852); 5F4L \"C5301 (HP3)\" (inputs/P.txt:676) is HETATM 5VE A501 C27, a ligand atom bonded 1.413 Angstrom to ligand hp2 C5281, not a protein point. Fig. 17 caption (inputs/P.txt:1165) lists hd1-HD1, hd1-HA1, hd2-HA2, HP-HP1, inconsistent with Sec. III.C labels, so the 5F4L optimum is tested only under a stated reading: hd1-HD1(N2206), hd1-HA2(O2209), hd3-HA4(O2479), HP contact omitted. Results ada/check_semantics_results.json:204-424. Crystal contact distances: 8SKH 2.931, 3.475, 3.250; 3HAC 2.753, 2.600, 2.709 and aromatic centroid 4.167; 5F4L 3.588, 3.694, 3.060 Angstrom. Hence the crystal pose itself violates ball semantics with P chosen \\(\\epsilon\\) in 3HAC and 5F4L under all allowances \\(\\epsilon\\), \\(\\epsilon+\\tau/2\\), \\(\\epsilon+\\tau\\), and in 8SKH except \\(\\epsilon+\\tau\\); it satisfies the window in 3HAC only. P \\(\\epsilon\\) therefore behaves as a pairwise tolerance, not a contact radius, which weakens the ball premise of #5/#15. Repeated-point enclosing radii are small (8SKH 2.357, 3HAC 2.385, 5F4L hd1 pair 1.340 Angstrom). Point-placement witnesses with rigid intraligand pharmacophore distances exist for all three under window semantics, and under ball semantics