  "emmy/heldout/final-account.txt": "Final joint account, working material.

The question has changed from transferring DCQAOA to thrombin non-additivity
to testing whether a declared contact representation has interpretable affinity
predictions before comparing optimisers. This is a potentially useful applied
audit, not an established publication result or quantum benefit.

Q reports coupling distributed over compensating interactions, with unresolved
hydration and entropy. Its F/B exception is explicit in
branches/branch-3/inputs/Q.txt:903-913. The SI central targets are recovered
in branches/branch-3/emmy/review-sources/q-si.txt:176-192,226-242:
\(\mathrm{NA}_{A/B}=-1.5\) and \(\mathrm{NA}_{F/B}=-1.6\) kcal/mol.
Negative NA corresponds to Q's positive coupling. P's weighted contact graph
is a different object, defined in branches/branch-3/inputs/P.txt:226-264;
neither solving it exactly nor preparing its optimum supplies a free energy.

Inherited branch evidence separates feature selection, receptor geometry,
contact domains, pose feasibility and optimiser residuals. Joint #3-6 and
ada/joint-account.txt:28-94 show that shared B sources must be retained and
that size normalisation can reverse affinity orientation. Different branch
rules are not independent replications of the same model.

The new branch-3 non-quartet calculation (joint #9-10) gives exact scores for
16 calibration ligands under a frozen four-receptor-feature representation.
Their source evidence and chemistry checks are inherited, not regenerated by
the final arithmetic. Within-series rank correlations are tolerance dependent.
The ligand rows are excluded from quartet calibration, but quartet inspection
informed earlier model work: these rows do not independently validate model
selection. No significance claim should rest on treating their B-relative
experimental uncertainties as independent.

Calibration correction, joint #17 and Ada #19:
\(y_L=b(W_L-W_{B,s})\) follows from a common ligand-level affine map;
the diagnostic free-series-intercept regression answers a different question.
At triples (3.1,2.5,0.1), (4,3,0.6), (2.8,2.5,0.1), the reference-respecting
slopes are -0.367149, -1.458310 and -3.693520. The primary predicted A/F
cycles are (+0.034842,+0.000367), (0,-0.549783), and
(-0.659663,-1.080355) kcal/mol. Thus claims of 5-7 per cent magnitude or
an approximately 1.14 kcal/mol upper bound apply only to the earlier centred
fit. Agreement with a target cycle after choosing calibration is not a rank
test and does not validate the representation. Snapshot scenario fractions
remain arithmetic counts rather than success probabilities. Recalibrating
with a different B source is another analysis; fixed-slope snapshot ranges
do not cover that procedure.

New descriptive prediction check, joint #18:
emmy/heldout/calibration_check.py:33-61 uses saved scores only. Each row's
prediction is fitted on the remaining 15, keeping A/F/B excluded. A training-only
per-series mean has RMSE 0.788437 kcal/mol. Free-series-intercept score models
have RMSE 0.827162, 0.724406 and 0.882167. Constraining their slope to the
intended decreasing orientation gives 0.827162, 0.788437 and 0.845425.
The improvement at the second triple is obtained only with the reversed
orientation. Reference-respecting through-origin fits give RMSE 2.014844,
1.897410 and 1.440081 despite their full-data negative slopes. These errors
are descriptive central-value diagnostics, not independent test estimates,
confidence intervals or proof of universal contact-model failure. The mean
comparator is a diagnostic of added score information, not a replacement
docking objective or a thermodynamic cycle predictor.

Executable evidence: emmy/heldout/calibration_check.py and
emmy/heldout/calibration-check-results.json. Input and script SHA256 values
are stored in the result. Centred closed-form regression checks the numerical
least-squares slopes, and the saved through-origin slopes agree. Ada #19
independently checks the calibration correction using Decimal arithmetic.
No archive code, new molecular simulation,
graph optimisation or quantum circuit was executed for this addition.

Practical decision: postpone quantum optimiser attribution for these declared
representations. A more accurately solved surrogate does not acquire missing
affinity information from optimisation alone. Publication value would require
chemically justified features and receptor domains, independently validated
endpoints and reconstructed feasible poses, a frozen calibration evaluated on
additional data, experimental covariance, and matched classical/quantum
resource accounting. The generic signed-cycle algebra is not itself novel.
Ada joint #20 additionally refits the slope for each shared B source choice.
emmy/heldout/refit_fraction_check.py independently reproduces its 600 tuples
per setting, both-negative counts 252,90,336 and every slope/cycle extreme
using exact fractions. The calibrated cycle ranges include target-scale values,
so negligible magnitudes are not a general obstruction. LOOCV above uses the
primary B sources only and does not reject every recalibrated alternative.
Joint scenario signs and marginal magnitudes do not establish simultaneous
quantitative target agreement. Source choices remain neither probabilities
nor samples from a physical ensemble.

Ada's separate v2-rule non-quartet test remains open. This account preserves
possible success of richer models instead of claiming all docking must fail.

Continuation (joint #31-32)

The primary-source restriction on the polar-model fold result is now removed
for the enumerated saved quartet-source domain. calibration_check.py explicitly
fits each excluded-row fold for all 600 shared-B tuples per setting, with no
A/F target entering calibration. refit_fraction_check.py checks all resulting
errors by exact Fraction arithmetic. The reference-origin and centred fits
remain different calibration questions.

The invariance has a direct explanation. Let \(x_L=W_L-W_{B,s}\).
Within each training fold, series centring gives
\[x_L-\overline{x}_{s,\mathrm{train}}
  = W_L-\overline{W}_{s,\mathrm{train}}.\]
Thus both the centred fitted slope and the held-row centred predictor are
independent of the common series reference. This holds also with the
constraint \(b\leq0\), since the constrained optimum is the unconstrained
slope clipped at zero. The baseline uses the remaining seven rows in the
held row's series, and the score fit uses all remaining fifteen rows.

At settings (3.1,2.5,0.1), (4,3,0.6), (2.8,2.5,0.1), decreasing centred
RMSEs are respectively 0.827162, 0.788437, 0.845425 kcal/mol versus the
