  "branches/branch-1/emmy/product_baseline.md": "# Product-distribution comparator for P

This is a supported algebraic derivation, conditional on independently adjustable gate angles. It is not a reproduction of P's optimisations and contains no molecular inputs.

P describes its numerical comparator as full-parameter QAOA (inputs/P.txt:312-331), with independently named RZZ, RZ and RX gates in Fig. 12 (inputs/P.txt:867-918). Its DC extension adds RY gates (inputs/P.txt:1124-1129). The rotation convention here is \(R_a(\theta)=\exp(-i\theta\sigma_a/2)\).

Set all RZZ angles to zero. For any desired bit \(x_i\), ordinary full-parameter QAOA can prepare that bit, up to phase, from \(|+\rangle\) with

\[
R_X(\pi/2)R_Z((1-2x_i)\pi/2)|+\rangle\propto|x_i\rangle.
\]

DC can instead set the cost and RX angles to zero and use

\[
R_Y((2x_i-1)\pi/2)|+\rangle=|x_i\rangle.
\]

Taking tensor products proves that both families contain every diagonal-objective ground-state bitstring at one layer. This is an existence result requiring knowledge of the desired assignment, not an efficient algorithm to discover it. It does not invalidate P's empirical optimisation observations. It changes their interpretation: observed depth dependence need not be an expressibility barrier.

For a minimisation objective specified independently of the ambiguous signs in P,

\[
E(x)=-\sum_iw_ix_i+\kappa\sum_{(i,j)\in\bar E,\ i<j}x_ix_j,
\]

an independent Bernoulli product distribution has the exact objective

\[
\mathcal E(q)=-\sum_iw_iq_i+\kappa\sum_{(i,j)\in\bar E,\ i<j}q_iq_j.
\]

The cost and gradient can be computed classically in \(O(N+|\bar E|)\) operations. Each coordinate enters affinely. Sequentially choosing its better endpoint cannot increase the cost and yields a deterministic assignment with cost no greater than the original product expectation. Global optimisation remains nonconvex. Removing entanglers and comparing classical optimisation of this expectation to P's full circuits is therefore a substantive ablation, rather than proof of quantum failure.

For a fair budget, count both objective and derivative evaluations, parameter numbers, starts, stopping criteria, preprocessing, circuit shots, and the QGT metric construction/inversion used by P (inputs/P.txt:937-951). Analytic access to full quantum amplitudes in simulation must be reported separately from hardware execution.

# Minimum scores do not identify an ensemble cycle

Q distinguishes relative binding free energy from the bound-minus-water alchemical terms (inputs/Q.txt:399-428), and reports mobility and unresolved entropy for the A quartet (inputs/Q.txt:1033-1063). Consider an illustrative bound-state model whose four minimum energies are identical. Give the 5F ligand two equal-energy bound microstates, and each other ligand one, with identical aqueous contributions and standard-state factors. Then

\[
\mathrm{NA}_{\min}=0,\qquad
\mathrm{NA}_{\mathrm{ensemble}}=-RT\ln2.
\]

At \(T=300\,\mathrm K\), this is approximately \(-0.41323\,\mathrm{kcal\,mol^{-1}}\). This is not a result for thrombin: it demonstrates a thermodynamic identifiability gap even after perfect minimum optimisation. Clique multiplicity is not automatically physical microstate multiplicity. Turning graph counts into a partition function would require physical state measures, calibrated energies, aqueous ensembles, and standard-state handling.

# Executable checks

Run the adjacent product_baseline.py with the project Python interpreter. It checks the single-qubit corner preparations, exact enumeration against the Bernoulli expectation on a fixed synthetic eight-variable QUBO, and monotone rounding on 100 seeded random product distributions. It also calculates the illustrative entropy-only cycle. No external libraries, biomolecular simulation, quantum hardware, or P-specific optimiser is used.
